  "status": "published",
  "title": "Live-Edge Coffee Table",
  "summary": "A walnut slab top flattened with a router sled and set on 16 in hairpin legs. Black-tinted epoxy fills the cracks.",
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedHours": 20,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Let the slab sit indoors for two weeks to settle to room humidity, then knock loose bark off the live edge with a chisel and wire brush.",
    "Build the sled: two straight 2x4 rails screwed to a frame longer than the slab, and a plywood carriage with the router mounted through a hole in its centre.",
    "Shim the slab on the bench so it cannot rock, set the router's depth to just touch the high spot, and take overlapping 1/16 in passes until the whole top is flat; flip and repeat on the bottom.",
    "Tape the underside of every crack with packing tape, mix the epoxy with a pinch of black pigment, and pour in layers no thicker than the label allows.",
    "After a full cure, rout or plane the epoxy flush, then sand the slab from 80 to 220 grit with the random orbit sander.",
    "Apply two thin coats of hardwax oil, buffing off the excess after each coat.",
    "Turn the top over on a blanket, mark the leg positions 3 in in from each edge, pre-drill, and screw the hairpin legs on with pan-head screws."
  ],
